                 ,
                  "0.11." : [
"
* Removed line breaks within logged messages
* Optimized the local configuration loading method
* Added a naming convention check for parameter names

  All parameter names under ``'params' : \u007b 'global': \u007b ... \u007d\u007d`` in the configuration file 
  are checked against the variable name rules of Robot Framework.

* Improved handling of duplicated parameters in configuration files:

  In following example different values are assigned to the same parameter several times. It can be seen that 
  the last assignment is the valid one. Previously assigned values will be overwritten. Please make sure to add
  the full scope in case you refer to a dictionary key inside this dictionary:

  | ``\u007b``
  |    ``\"params\" : \u007b``
  |         ``\"global\" : \u007b``
  |              ``\"var1\" : 1,``
  |              ``\"var2\" : 2,``
  |              ``\"var1\" : $\u007bparams.global.var2\u007d,``
  |              ``\"var2\" : 3``
  |         ``\u007d``
  |    ``\u007d``
  | ``\u007d``

  Finally, the value of ``var1`` is 2 and the value of ``var2`` is 3.

* Extended debugging support:

  In case of JSON syntax errors in configuration files, the **Robot Framework** log files contain an extract of the JSON content nearby the position, where the error occurred.
  Line breaks in JSON content are replaced by '``\\n``' in log output.

  | ``ERROR - Expecting ',' delimiter: line 10 column 1 (char 246)``
  | ``ERROR - Nearby: '... arams\" : \u007b\\n              \"global\": \u007b\\n\"param1\" : 1\\n\"param2\" : 2\\n        ...'``

"
                            ]
                 ,
                 "0.12.1.;0.13.0.;0.13.1.;0.13.2.;0.14.0." : [
"
* Improved error messages in case of issues while loading the test configuration.

* Updated JSON schema validation.

* Resolved redundant code.

* Modified the naming convention check to be aligned with Robot Framework core standards.

* Extended import of variant configuration files

  After the **RobotFramework_TestsuitesManagement** library is imported

  | ``Library\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0RobotFramework_TestsuitesManagement\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0WITH\u00a0NAME\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0testsuites``

  a variant configuration file is usually loaded in a way like this:

  | ``Suite\u00a0Setup\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0testsuites.testsuite_setup\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0./config/variants_config.jsonp``

  With the *three-dots* syntax extension for relative paths, the positional dependency between the file containing the suite setup and the file with the variant configuration, is made dynamic.

  | ``Suite\u00a0Setup\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0testsuites.testsuite_setup\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0.../config/variants_config.jsonp``

  The Robot Framework now also recursively considers parent directories while searching for variant configuration files starting with the actual relative path.

  **Important**: This feature previously was located **inside** the variant configuration files, e.g.:

  | ``\"default\": \u007b``
  | ``  \"name\": \"testconfig.json\",``
  | ``  \"path\": \".../\"``
  | ``\u007d``

  **This is not supported any more!** Only relative paths to the actual directory (``\"path\": \"./\"``) and to the parent directory (``\"path\": \"../\"``) are possible.

  **Why this change?**

  **Reason 1**: It would be confusing to have the *three-dots* syntax available at two different positions in parallel in the code.

  **Reason 2**: It is possible now to move the folder containing the variant configuration files and the parameter configuration files up and down in the hierarchy of directories in the file sytem
  **without the need to adapt any import path afterwards**!
"
                           ]
                  ,
                  "0.14.1.;0.15.0." : [
"
**Updated naming convention check**

  Keys that are defined within ``params['global']`` in JSONP configuration files become **Robot Framework** variables. Therefore, the key names must conform to **Robot Framework**'s naming conventions.

  The naming convention is: Key names may only contain letters, digits and underscores, and must start with a letter.
  Internally this is realized by the following regex pattern:

  ``^[\\p{L}][\\p{L}0-9_]*$``

  *Examples*

  * Valid variable names are: ``\"abcParam\"``, ``\"abcParam01\"``, ``\"abc_Param_01\"``, ...
  * Invalid variable names are: ``\"+param01\"``, ``\"param$01\"``, ``\"abc#Param\"``, ``\"01abc_Param\"``, ...

**Maintenance**

* Extended logging of test results
* Replaced ``re`` package by ``regex`` package
"
                  ]
